  <div slot="started">
    <p>
      John was LSR’s first client, reached through early Facebook prospecting when Alistair was still
      learning Google Ads from scratch. On that first call, John wasn’t shopping features. He was in
      the middle of a brutal stretch: an office betrayal and firing, running jobs himself, and years of
      paying marketers who never made advertising profitable. He talked for the better part of an hour
      before the real ask landed: <em>want to give it a go?</em>
    </p>
    <p>
      The honest pitch was simple: low budget, maximum effort, no fake certainty. John had already
      tried something like five or six companies. One more experiment wasn’t optimism, it was
      necessity.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What almost ended the relationship (and what saved it)</h2>
  <p>
    The hard parts weren’t only campaigns. They were the moments bigger agencies promised a cleaner
    website and “best in class” systems, then killed the organic channel that was paying the bills
    and couldn’t restart paid. One rebuild soaked tens of thousands of dollars and months of weaker
    lead flow before John pulled ads back.
  </p>
  <p>
    What kept the partnership alive was a simple rule: if someone else can do it better, they should.
    Alistair offered to step aside, stay on as a light consultant for context, and return only if the
    new team couldn’t deliver. They couldn’t. That humility, plus actually making ads print money,
    is why Rewired stopped cycling agencies and kept one partner for the long haul.
  </p>
  <p>
    Along the way the work also meant owner support when stress and paranoia ran high in year one.
    Clear expectations are easier now. Back then it was problem-solving under pressure until both
    sides got stronger.
  </p>
